WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.568
I started freelancing in 2014,

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.602
and it's been over ten years now.

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.436
People often ask me,

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:05.038
how do you manage your time?

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:05.271
Well,

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:05.672
to be honest,

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:06.473
like many others,

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:07.907
especially in the beginning,

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.743
I found time management incredibly challenging.

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:10.477
Because,

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:12.645
when you suddenly have a lot of time on your hands,

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:16.282
you realize you were previously managed by your company's schedule.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:17.183
Once that framework is gone,

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:18.985
you just don't know what to do.

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:20.053
But after all these years,

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:21.354
struggling and learning,

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:22.422
my current state is that

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:24.190
I've become very used to it.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.962
So today, I'll just make a video to share how I manage my time.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.397
Whether you're a freelancer or not,

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:32.966
there are many principles and methodologies here

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:34.401
that I believe will be very practical for you.

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:34.968
This sharing,

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.002
will be divided into two parts.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.971
The first part is about changes in mindset,

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:43.643
and the second part is about specific methods and principles.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:45.678
If you search for time management on Xiaohongshu,

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.147
you'll see many,

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:49.716
uh, extremely beautiful calendars and to-do lists,

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.818
with every minute filled in tightly.

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:54.421
At first, I also thought that was what time management was,

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:55.355
but after 10 years,

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:56.122
I found that

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.391
these perfectly drawn calendars

00:00:58.425 --> 00:00:59.859
can only mean two things.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:00.827
First, they are fake,

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.095
just a demo for you.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:04.297
Second, if someone really does that,

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:06.766
uh, then it's highly likely they're just busy for the sake of being busy.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.635
My biggest shift in understanding is right here.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:11.371
True time management doesn't really exist at all,

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:13.406
you're not managing time,

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.908
but your attention.

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:16.209
Those jam-packed schedules,

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:18.645
are essentially treating time as fuel,

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:22.081
believing that an hour invested will yield an hour's worth of results.

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:23.983
But humans aren't machines,

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:25.452
if you're a normal person,

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:30.757
then the truly efficient time you can spend in a state of focused flow each day is probably only around four hours.

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:31.524
Once you understand this,

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:33.626
you won't feel like eating,

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:34.194
cooking,

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:35.261
or sleeping is a waste of time anymore,

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.864
because you might not have been able to make use of that time effectively anyway.

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:41.801
So, since your high-energy attention span is only that long,

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.437
then planning a millisecond-level schedule is misguided.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:48.541
Time management should change with the variations in your attention.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.544
When we talk about managing attention,

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.346
we must admit,

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.347
uh, it has two characteristics.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:54.581
First,

00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:55.915
attention isn't uniform.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:56.716
For me,

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:59.819
the time from waking up to before lunch is when my attention is best,

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:00.687
but by evening,

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:01.588
my brain is already scattered,

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:03.857
and it's hard to focus. This is like the Tian Ji's Horse Racing strategy,

00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:06.059
if I invest time when my attention is at its peak,

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.494
I might have several times the output,

00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:09.562
but if I stay up late when I'm tired,

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:10.530
not only is the efficiency low,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.133
it's a wasteful effort that doesn't pay off.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:13.766
Second,

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:16.135
where your attention is directed determines your results.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:16.936
As I said before,

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.905
if you want to surpass 99% of people,

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.708
you have to put your attention on what's most important to you.

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.309
So, what are the most important things?

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.711
Uh, if you want to win a Nobel Prize,

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:27.714
then you should focus your attention on the problems that can help you win that prize.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.783
So if you expend all your precious attention on trivial matters,

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.085
even if your time management is,

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:34.420
uh, perfectly flawless, it's meaningless.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.658
This brings me to my second cognitive shift, which might sound a bit funny,

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:44.430
and that is, I finally understood the difference between a calendar and a to-do list.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:45.198
(Sound effect) So in the past,

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:46.399
uh, I would put all my tasks,

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:48.101
into this calendar,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.636
and draw out the blocks,

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:52.105
because according to time management principles,

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:55.208
uh, of course, everything should have a start and end time.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:56.342
While this looks good on the surface,

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:58.878
what you gain more of is a sense of frustration.

00:02:58.878 --> 00:02:59.879
Because for the most important tasks,

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:00.680
for example,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.347
uh, writing a script,

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:03.283
or creative work, it's often outcome-oriented,

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.718
and it's hard to dictate that it must be finished within half an hour.

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:08.288
We often underestimate the time we need to spend,

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:12.158
In reality, most of our problems aren't about going overtime on important tasks,

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:16.796
but rather underestimating or deliberately avoiding spending time on important tasks.

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:21.334
This then leads to a situation where if you can't complete something within the time you've scheduled on your calendar,

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.802
your entire day's plan gets completely disrupted.

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:23.570
Therefore,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:29.275
my principle now is very simple: the calendar only records things that must happen at a scheduled time,

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:30.777
for example, doctor's appointments,

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:31.377
catching a flight,

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:32.211
or meetings with others.

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:34.447
As for reminders and to-dos,

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:36.749
I put all other tasks there,

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:37.750
and for these to-dos,

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.587
I try to avoid setting specific due times.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:40.954
Alright,

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:43.890
So, having discussed the cognitive shifts in time management,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.060
let's now talk about how I specifically allocate my time,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.695
and when executing tasks,

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:50.063
the specific methods and mindset.

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:53.132
First, let me talk about my time allocation.

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:53.833
Firstly,

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:57.470
I will do my utmost to protect my mornings.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.972
Since my energy is at its peak in the mornings,

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:02.508
I dedicate this time to creative work.

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.177
My protection of my morning time,

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:06.546
has reached an almost paranoid level.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:07.814
I don't schedule meetings,

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:08.748
don't go grocery shopping,

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:09.449
don't see doctors,

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.717
I'm even unwilling to talk to anyone,

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:12.252
to say an extra word,

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:13.486
and certainly won't check messages,

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:14.153
or reply to them,

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.289
because that consumes my attention reserves.

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:16.823
Specifically,

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:18.458
I divide my mornings into two main blocks.

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:20.660
Uh, the first block is the 5 AM Project,

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:21.127
from waking up,

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:22.895
and finishing breakfast until 8:30 AM.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.097
Those familiar with my content know that

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:25.231
I wake up very early every day,

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.600
and have a 5 AM Project.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.201
The concept behind it is,

00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:28.868
to utilize

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:31.337
the least disturbed time of the day,

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:33.039
when the world is still asleep,

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:33.573
to do things that are

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:35.008
most important to me,

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:35.742
most interesting,

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:37.043
and what I most want to do.

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:37.644
For example, recently,

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:41.414
I've been using AI every morning to develop an app I've always wanted to create.

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:44.417
Let me interject here,

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:45.618
if you'd also like to try this,

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.854
you're welcome to join our community's Five Club.

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:49.722
Seeing everyone check in daily there,

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:50.890
and update all sorts of projects,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.259
that atmosphere is truly incredibly motivating.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:54.961
The detailed link is below the video.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:56.663
Now, the second main block of time in the morning,

00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:59.098
is after the 5 AM Project ends,

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:00.233
after 8:30 AM.

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:01.701
that's when I start doing,

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:03.102
uh, YouTube content creation,

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:03.703
editing,

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:04.303
or,

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:06.506
the core work of new startup projects.

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:07.206
Speaking of which,

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:08.107
I must,

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:10.410
uh, also add a crucial mindset,

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.111
which is that you will often,

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:14.180
uh, hear various bloggers say,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.683
'This is how I spend every day.'

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:19.052
These are all illusions.

00:05:19.052 --> 00:05:21.387
Because I've been a freelancer for ten years,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.789
I can tell you responsibly,

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:26.726
it's impossible to live every day by the exact same routine.

00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:27.093
For example,

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:28.428
sometimes I have jet lag issues,

00:05:28.428 --> 00:05:29.729
and have to attend meetings early in the morning,

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:33.800
or have to sacrifice a 5-AM project to do another urgent and important task.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.235
Life happens, you'll always have surprises,

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:37.437
uh, unexpected situations,

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:38.304
you'll travel,

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:39.305
your projects will change,

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.607
or you might simply be tired and want to rest.

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:45.578
If you pursue that kind of perfectionism where every day is perfectly planned,

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:48.047
you will definitely fall into deep frustration.

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:50.850
So don't force yourself to be perfect every day.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.785
Compared to guaranteeing a full day's routine,

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:55.722
guaranteeing a routine for key time slots is much easier,

00:05:55.722 --> 00:05:56.856
and much more realistic.

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:57.890
For me,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.225
even when traveling,

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:01.527
even if the rest of my day is chaotic,

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:05.331
as long as I can stick to the period from 5 AM to 8:30 AM,

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:08.935
stick to the habit of doing the most important things before the world wakes up,

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.503
then I've already won.

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:13.806
As long as you can put your best energy into the most important tasks most of the time,

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:14.474
that's enough.

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:15.808
After finishing the intense morning work,

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:17.110
I'll go for a walk,

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:17.744
Cooking.

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:17.910
Ha,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.613
I know many freelancers feel that cooking,

00:06:20.613 --> 00:06:22.048
and doing chores are a waste of time.

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:23.549
But as I said at the beginning of my video,

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:25.518
you can't possibly utilize every minute of your attention.

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:27.353
So don't fret over how much opportunity cost you've lost because of cooking,

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:29.455
or doing chores,

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.190
because those opportunities never existed.

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:32.525
After lunch, I do some chores,

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:33.259
to digest my food.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:36.028
Then I'll have a fifteen to twenty-minute power nap,

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:36.696
a midday nap.

00:06:36.696 --> 00:06:39.732
For the old me, who used to plan time down to the minute,

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:42.401
I always thought (Laughter) napping was a bad habit.

00:06:42.401 --> 00:06:43.236
But after a cognitive shift,

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:46.038
I realized it's a miracle worker for rebooting the brain.

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:48.741
When you wake up, it's like a new day has begun.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:51.411
However, this time needs to be strictly controlled to within twenty minutes.

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:54.080
If you sleep longer, you'll actually feel more tired when you wake up.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.018
In the afternoon, I generally finish up any important tasks that are still incomplete.

00:06:59.018 --> 00:06:59.418
And then,

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:01.053
I will focus on handling those,

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:02.522
non-creative tasks,

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:03.022
such as,

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:04.357
some administrative work,

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:04.891
meetings,

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:06.592
replying to emails, and other miscellaneous things,

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:07.727
including exercise.

00:07:07.727 --> 00:07:09.729
Since I mentioned batch processing,

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:10.930
I want to say a few more words,

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:11.464
because,

00:07:11.464 --> 00:07:14.667
uh, it's a very important method in my time management.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:17.670
My batch processing, then,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.071
is divided into two situations.

00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:24.744
The first is batch processing a project that requires full dedication, on a daily basis.

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:25.244
For example,

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:28.247
I dedicate three days each year to complete tax filing.

00:07:28.247 --> 00:07:29.849
And for shooting my YouTube videos,

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:32.885
I often dedicate an entire day to it.

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:33.686
New projects,

00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:36.255
and the launch of new websites often involve a few days of focused sprints,

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:39.792
because many of these projects require momentum,

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.228
or the setup preparation is very tedious,

00:07:42.228 --> 00:07:44.263
so centralized processing is more efficient.

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:45.431
The second type of centralized processing,

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:47.667
is concentrating small blocks of time within a day.

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:49.769
For example, when I'm not in good shape in the afternoon,

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:51.604
I will gather small time slots,

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:52.805
half an hour or an hour,

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:53.706
to process in one go,

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:55.475
uh, those tedious tasks,

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:57.710
that I don't really want to do but have to do.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:58.578
For instance,

00:07:58.578 --> 00:07:59.812
uh, paying a bill,

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:00.646
filling out a form,

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:01.180
replying to an email,

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.648
making a phone call, and so on.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.584
I have to process these things in a concentrated manner,

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:06.452
because once I process them separately,

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:08.387
my attention and time will be scattered.

00:08:08.421 --> 00:08:08.955
Okay,

00:08:08.955 --> 00:08:12.391
what I just mentioned were methods for large-scale time planning.

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:17.363
Now, let's talk about the methods and principles I use when executing specific tasks.

00:08:19.265 --> 00:08:20.666
The first thing I want to share is,

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:23.803
uh, the three maxims for single-task processing.

00:08:23.803 --> 00:08:28.307
I am a firm believer in single-tasking, or rather, sequential tasking.

00:08:28.307 --> 00:08:30.409
Multitasking is a false premise for me,

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:32.378
because that way I don't do anything well.

00:08:32.378 --> 00:08:35.648
The person who influenced me most on this point is Elon Musk.

00:08:35.648 --> 00:08:37.617
His biographer,

00:08:37.617 --> 00:08:37.750
uh,

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:41.354
talked about Elon's unique time management approach in an interview.

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:41.787
He said,

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:43.890
Musk is not a multi-tasker,

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.992
but a sequential tasker.

00:08:45.992 --> 00:08:47.660
He focuses on one thing at a time,

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:49.395
and then switches to the next.

00:08:49.395 --> 00:08:50.563
But that's not the key point,

00:08:50.563 --> 00:08:52.131
what impressed me even more is,

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:54.267
Uh, when Elon Musk handles a task,

00:08:54.267 --> 00:08:56.636
it's as if he builds four walls around himself,

00:08:56.636 --> 00:08:57.904
completely isolating it.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.272
The author also shared a story

00:08:59.272 --> 00:09:00.840
that vividly illustrates this point.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.809
On the very day Musk successfully acquired Twitter,

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:06.946
he immediately delved into SpaceX's rocket research.

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:09.615
Employees knew that buying Twitter was a huge deal,

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:11.751
and were wondering if they should congratulate him.

00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:13.953
But Musk was focused on the rockets,

00:09:13.953 --> 00:09:16.122
as if he had forgotten all about acquiring Twitter.

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:20.960
I call this time management method 'faith-based single-tasking'.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.729
This is a method I've been using for several years,

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:24.997
and to achieve it,

00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:28.334
I have three simple and memorable mantras.

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:29.201
First,

00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:31.070
I've developed a conditioned reflex:

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.940
when I'm about to start a task or when it's its turn,

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.642
I immediately think of the word 'attack',

00:09:36.642 --> 00:09:39.712
making immediate action the first reflex when thinking of a task.

00:09:40.112 --> 00:09:41.614
There's no intermediate preparation process,

00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:43.749
and this is very effective for treating procrastination,

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:46.319
because once you start doing something,

00:09:46.319 --> 00:09:49.388
a large part of your previous worries and fears about it disappear,

00:09:49.388 --> 00:09:51.891
and this also helps you quickly enter a state of flow,

00:09:51.891 --> 00:09:53.225
maintaining focus for extended periods.

00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:54.961
Second,

00:09:54.961 --> 00:09:56.028
when starting a task,

00:09:56.028 --> 00:09:57.897
I silently repeat to myself,

00:09:57.897 --> 00:09:58.464
isolate,

00:09:58.464 --> 00:09:59.031
isolate,

00:09:59.031 --> 00:09:59.565
and isolate again.

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:02.335
I imagine building four walls around the task,

00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:07.606
fully committing my time and attention to it. Forcing myself to forget about other things.

00:10:08.941 --> 00:10:09.408
Third,

00:10:09.408 --> 00:10:12.445
when I feel distracted or someone tries to interrupt me,

00:10:12.445 --> 00:10:13.679
I immediately say, "Hey,

00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:14.580
we'll talk about it later.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.015
No thinking, no discussing now,

00:10:16.015 --> 00:10:17.850
no worrying." And this phrase,

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.353
helps me bring my focus back to the current task,

00:10:20.353 --> 00:10:21.520
avoiding distractions.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.556
I know this might sound a bit annoying,

00:10:23.556 --> 00:10:23.723
uh,

00:10:23.723 --> 00:10:25.958
but if you find yourself saying this often,

00:10:25.958 --> 00:10:28.527
it means you are consciously protecting your attention.

00:10:28.527 --> 00:10:30.663
As long as you're doing the most important thing,

00:10:30.663 --> 00:10:33.165
other things can actually be delayed,

00:10:33.165 --> 00:10:34.567
or even treated as non-existent.

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:36.736
Okay,

00:10:36.736 --> 00:10:38.504
so these are my methods for working on tasks.

00:10:38.504 --> 00:10:41.674
Now I'll share my methods for switching tasks.

00:10:43.275 --> 00:10:44.143
For a long time,

00:10:44.143 --> 00:10:47.413
I didn't understand why some people liked working in cafes,

00:10:47.413 --> 00:10:48.247
with people coming and going,

00:10:48.247 --> 00:10:48.748
and it being particularly noisy.

00:10:48.748 --> 00:10:50.383
How could one be efficient there?

00:10:50.383 --> 00:10:52.184
I always maintained that,

00:10:52.184 --> 00:10:52.284
uh,

00:10:52.284 --> 00:10:54.687
work should be done properly, sitting at a desk,

00:10:54.687 --> 00:10:55.821
for the entire day.

00:10:55.821 --> 00:10:57.256
Sitting on the sofa,

00:10:57.256 --> 00:10:57.923
in the living room,

00:10:57.923 --> 00:10:58.591
at the dining table,

00:10:58.591 --> 00:11:01.494
or on the beach, etc., wasn't serious work.

00:11:01.494 --> 00:11:03.996
It wasn't until I read some neuroscience materials

00:11:03.996 --> 00:11:06.599
that I realized I was completely wrong.

00:11:06.599 --> 00:11:06.666
Uh,

00:11:06.666 --> 00:11:09.602
psychology has a concept called 'attention residue',

00:11:09.602 --> 00:11:12.038
which means when you switch from task A to task B,

00:11:12.038 --> 00:11:13.906
your brain often remains stuck on task A,

00:11:13.906 --> 00:11:17.143
making it difficult for you to truly immerse yourself in the new task.

00:11:17.143 --> 00:11:18.411
This made me realize that,

00:11:18.411 --> 00:11:19.912
to solve this problem,

00:11:19.912 --> 00:11:23.849
we should make switching tasks equivalent to switching environments.

00:11:23.849 --> 00:11:24.550
This is because,

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.819
changes in environment have a huge impact on the brain,

00:11:26.819 --> 00:11:29.522
and a change of environment can bring a sense of novelty,

00:11:29.522 --> 00:11:32.091
effectively helping the brain switch attention,

00:11:32.091 --> 00:11:33.259
and rejuvenate itself.

00:11:33.259 --> 00:11:36.395
And staying in the same place for a long time, like I used to,

00:11:36.395 --> 00:11:39.532
makes it especially easy to fall into this 'attention residue',

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:39.732
uh,

00:11:39.732 --> 00:11:41.100
making myself very inefficient.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:42.301
After understanding this principle,

00:11:42.301 --> 00:11:43.836
my working method completely changed.

00:11:43.836 --> 00:11:45.371
I no longer rigidly,

00:11:45.371 --> 00:11:48.074
sitting at a desk like a student.

00:11:48.074 --> 00:11:49.308
Every time I switch tasks,

00:11:49.308 --> 00:11:50.576
I'll change my environment.

00:11:50.576 --> 00:11:51.444
For example,

00:11:51.444 --> 00:11:52.411
I'll go to the sofa,

00:11:52.411 --> 00:11:54.046
from the study on the second floor to the living room on the first floor,

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:54.747
then to the backyard,

00:11:54.747 --> 00:11:55.815
then to the library,

00:11:55.815 --> 00:11:57.116
or even go for a walk.

00:11:57.116 --> 00:11:57.416
Yes,

00:11:57.416 --> 00:12:00.052
outdoor walks can also be your workplace.

00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:02.488
Many of my video scripts were written while walking.

00:12:02.688 --> 00:12:03.756
So you see,

00:12:03.756 --> 00:12:03.989
uh,

00:12:03.989 --> 00:12:08.861
if the word 'attention' makes you think of 'immutability',

00:12:08.861 --> 00:12:11.997
then the key to switching attention is 'change'.

00:12:11.997 --> 00:12:15.434
So changing the environment is a very good method.

00:12:15.434 --> 00:12:15.835
Okay,

00:12:15.835 --> 00:12:19.238
those were the methods I've been using for the past few years.

00:12:19.238 --> 00:12:19.705
But,

00:12:19.705 --> 00:12:20.439
in the past year,

00:12:20.439 --> 00:12:23.309
my time management has added a new rule,

00:12:23.309 --> 00:12:27.246
which is setting a forced shutdown time.

00:12:28.948 --> 00:12:30.883
This concept of a forced shutdown,

00:12:30.883 --> 00:12:33.919
I first heard about it from a YouTuber I really like,

00:12:33.919 --> 00:12:36.021
named Johnny Harris.

00:12:36.021 --> 00:12:36.388
He said,

00:12:36.388 --> 00:12:39.091
because every day around 4 PM,

00:12:39.091 --> 00:12:40.826
he and his wife have to take care of their child,

00:12:40.826 --> 00:12:43.362
so he has to race against time before then,

00:12:43.362 --> 00:12:45.164
to get all the work he can done.

00:12:45.164 --> 00:12:46.699
His 'forced shutdown',

00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:47.767
is not something he actively chose,

00:12:47.767 --> 00:12:49.335
but was forced to implement it.

00:12:49.335 --> 00:12:52.271
However, he found that it was actually very efficient this way.

00:12:52.271 --> 00:12:53.305
When I first heard this concept,

00:12:53.305 --> 00:12:53.839
I thought,

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:54.173
hmm,

00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:55.241
I also wanted to implement it,

00:12:55.241 --> 00:12:58.310
but I didn't have his insurmountable factors.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:12:58.511
Uh,

00:12:58.511 --> 00:13:00.980
I was never able to successfully implement it,

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:03.182
leading to me often staying up late.

00:13:03.182 --> 00:13:05.451
This is the biggest problem for many freelancers,

00:13:05.451 --> 00:13:08.654
which is the lack of a so-called work-life balance.

00:13:08.654 --> 00:13:09.688
But since last year,

00:13:09.688 --> 00:13:13.292
I suddenly became able to successfully implement this forced shutdown schedule.

00:13:13.292 --> 00:13:15.127
That's because of my heart condition.

00:13:15.127 --> 00:13:16.962
I have to take medication twice a day.

00:13:16.962 --> 00:13:18.831
8:30 AM and 8:30 PM.

00:13:18.831 --> 00:13:21.767
So I gave the act of taking medicine a sense of ritual.

00:13:21.834 --> 00:13:22.935
Taking medicine at 8:30 AM

00:13:22.935 --> 00:13:25.337
reminds me that the 5 AM project must end,

00:13:25.337 --> 00:13:26.472
and it's time to get down to serious business.

00:13:29.808 --> 00:13:32.011
Then taking medicine at 8:30 PM tells me

00:13:32.011 --> 00:13:33.345
I should stop working,

00:13:33.345 --> 00:13:34.580
and force a shutdown.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:36.081
No matter how big the matter is, I won't work on it,

00:13:36.081 --> 00:13:37.483
otherwise, I'll kick the bucket.

00:13:37.483 --> 00:13:38.184
So,

00:13:38.184 --> 00:13:42.054
this unexpected force majeure has brought me great benefits.

00:13:42.054 --> 00:13:42.855
For over a year now,

00:13:42.855 --> 00:13:44.023
I haven't stayed up late.

00:13:44.023 --> 00:13:44.223
Uh,

00:13:44.223 --> 00:13:46.058
most of the time I go to bed at 9:30 PM,

00:13:46.058 --> 00:13:48.194
and wake up naturally at 5 AM,

00:13:48.194 --> 00:13:49.395
starting my 5 AM project.

00:13:49.395 --> 00:13:50.429
And my physical condition?

00:13:50.429 --> 00:13:51.363
It's very good.

00:13:51.363 --> 00:13:55.701
Although it objectively reduced the number of things I can do,

00:13:55.701 --> 00:13:57.636
and sometimes I feel a little anxious,

00:13:57.636 --> 00:13:59.738
it ensures my long-term sustainability,

00:13:59.738 --> 00:14:03.275
and forces me to consciously spend my time wisely.

00:14:03.275 --> 00:14:06.712
and adopt better methods and tools to make yourself more efficient.

00:14:06.745 --> 00:14:10.015
So, that pretty much sums up my experience with time management over my many years as a freelancer.

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:12.551
Of course, there are many specific operational details

00:14:12.551 --> 00:14:13.886
that I can't share one by one.

00:14:13.886 --> 00:14:17.489
However, I believe that by mastering the core concepts in this video,

00:14:17.489 --> 00:14:21.627
you should also be able to find specific methods and tools that suit you.

00:14:21.627 --> 00:14:22.394
I know,

00:14:22.394 --> 00:14:26.398
time management, or rather attention management, is a challenge for many of us,

00:14:26.398 --> 00:14:27.866
especially with smartphones,

00:14:27.866 --> 00:14:28.534
social media,

00:14:28.534 --> 00:14:29.702
and short videos,

00:14:29.702 --> 00:14:29.802
uh,

00:14:29.802 --> 00:14:31.904
it's become even harder to concentrate.

00:14:31.904 --> 00:14:32.438
So, here,

00:14:32.438 --> 00:14:35.708
I recommend everyone read a book,

00:14:35.774 --> 00:14:37.977
uh, called Stolen Focus.

00:14:37.977 --> 00:14:42.014
This book brilliantly explores why we lose focus so easily,

00:14:42.014 --> 00:14:45.284
and specifically how to regain our attention.

00:14:45.351 --> 00:14:46.585
This book is also

00:14:46.585 --> 00:14:49.521
the book club selection for the December Yikou Xinfan community.

00:14:49.521 --> 00:14:50.522
If you're interested,

00:14:50.522 --> 00:14:51.790
feel free to join us.

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:52.291
Alright,

00:14:52.291 --> 00:14:53.325
thank you for watching.

00:14:53.325 --> 00:14:54.226
We'll see you next time.

00:14:54.226 --> 00:14:54.493
Bye-bye!

00:14:54.493 --> 00:14:55.027
(Outro music)

